WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:03.303
When students go to university
to study mathematics,

00:00:03.536 --> 00:00:08.041
the undergraduate fantasy that we teach
is that we have this bedrock of axioms

00:00:08.041 --> 00:00:12.178
and everything is built solely on
these axioms through deductive argument,

00:00:12.278 --> 00:00:16.116
and that we have a towering edifice
of brilliant mathematics,

00:00:16.116 --> 00:00:21.354
all of which is solidly established
in an incontrovertible way.

00:00:21.421 --> 00:00:23.390
it's a beautiful kind of conception,

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:24.624
the problem is that's not true.

00:00:24.624 --> 00:00:27.917
It's not even close to the truth.

00:00:27.917 --> 00:00:29.996
It's almost impossible
to live up to that fantasy.

00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:33.366
Some of the top mathematicians
in the world have been looking

00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:34.667
at this as a philosophy question,

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:37.670
I've been actually working with A.I.

00:00:37.804 --> 00:00:40.473
If you start always inputting your ideas

00:00:40.473 --> 00:00:42.575
or proofs into a machine,
at what point does

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:46.546
the machine do a better job of helping
guess the next step?

00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:49.649
Obviously this is something very 
hot right now.

00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:54.421
It's moving fast
and the question becomes, well, what is it

00:00:54.421 --> 00:00:56.260
we want from proofs?

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:58.558
What have we
historically needed from proofs?

00:00:58.658 --> 00:01:01.027
What is it we believe when something's proved

00:01:01.027 --> 00:01:03.139
and how does AI change that?

00:01:03.139 --> 00:01:08.635
These are massive questions that that are popping
out of the woodwork right now.

00:01:08.735 --> 00:01:10.103
I'm Andrew Granville,

00:01:10.103 --> 00:01:12.063
I work in analytic number theory.

00:01:12.063 --> 00:01:15.575
When I was young. I worked on Fermat's
Last Theorem before it was proved.

00:01:15.575 --> 00:01:19.212
Today, I work in ideas of L functions
and multiplicative functions.

00:01:19.345 --> 00:01:22.348
But I'm interested
in all different aspects,

00:01:22.348 --> 00:01:26.252
including computational
and algorithmic questions.

00:01:26.352 --> 00:01:29.689
I've long been interested in popular
writing in mathematics.

00:01:29.856 --> 00:01:31.611
My sister is a writer.

00:01:31.611 --> 00:01:35.081
We've been thinking
it would be fun to do a graphic novel in mathematics,

00:01:35.081 --> 00:01:38.631
so we worked together
to develop it and eventually wrote it.

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:41.868
In the graphic novel,
I felt like there was room

00:01:41.868 --> 00:01:45.605
for us to develop philosophy,

00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:51.519
A philosopher of mathematics. Michael Hallett, read it and he was very interested in

00:01:51.519 --> 00:01:55.115
the way we portrayed mathematics being done.

00:01:55.215 --> 00:01:58.384
You cannot prove them
because you cannot go back

00:01:58.384 --> 00:02:03.089
to anything more primitive
than those propositions themselves,

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:06.192
they are not in need of justification.

00:02:06.226 --> 00:02:08.895
Talking to Michael,
I realized there is this debate

00:02:08.895 --> 00:02:15.435
of what it is we mean
when we say we've got something proved.

00:02:15.535 --> 00:02:16.503
So Aristotle

00:02:16.503 --> 00:02:20.473
said that to prove
something is true, to establish it's true,

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:23.009
your argument should rest on what

00:02:23.009 --> 00:02:26.012
he called primitives, things
you already knew to be true.

00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:28.882
And eventually there's got to be some atoms there,

00:02:28.882 --> 00:02:31.818
the so-called axioms
that you rest all your arguments on.

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:35.522
the basic things you cannot define,
so like point, line,

00:02:35.522 --> 00:02:38.725
plane, if you're talking about geometry,
you can't define those.

00:02:38.725 --> 00:02:43.163
What you do instead is lay down
some axioms about them.

00:02:43.396 --> 00:02:46.833
The phrase that comes to my mind
is these truths are self-evident.

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:50.069
Um, which, you know, is interesting at many levels

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:51.671
when you compare it to mathematics.

00:02:51.671 --> 00:02:54.674
I mean, there's a certain arrogance
about it.

00:02:54.874 --> 00:02:57.844
The way it's traditionally been done
is that we publish papers and there's

00:02:57.844 --> 00:03:01.681
papers that put in journals or books,
and those books are put in libraries.

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:05.418
And then when we wanted to verify a truth,
we go to the library

00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:08.988
and we check out the book and we'd
see if it was exactly as we wanted it.

00:03:09.088 --> 00:03:09.923
It would help us gain

00:03:09.923 --> 00:03:13.993
new understanding, or we'd have to revise
what was in those books.

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:17.897
Today, The artificial intelligence
that is used for proof

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:21.601
they're doing something similar, except
they're going to store that information

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:24.103
within the program.

00:03:24.204 --> 00:03:27.207
So programs
like Lean have a library of things

00:03:27.207 --> 00:03:31.344
already proven based on axioms
and then with suitable work,

00:03:31.344 --> 00:03:33.012
and it's complicated,

00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:35.949
a mathematician can input their proof
into Lean and verify

00:03:35.949 --> 00:03:39.219
all the steps of logical,
at least according to Lean.

00:03:39.319 --> 00:03:40.587
So what does Lean do?

00:03:40.587 --> 00:03:45.525
I think I have a proof of a theorem
and I want a formal proof.

00:03:45.625 --> 00:03:48.628
I've written something that I am a bit
iffy about.

00:03:48.795 --> 00:03:52.765
I don't want to get into all of the details
of the ins and outs of know, as you

00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:56.502
sort of go back to the Tree of Knowledge,
everything's really going to work.

00:03:56.603 --> 00:03:59.806
So the idea is to input that into Lean.

00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:04.844
It acts like an obnoxious colleague
who just won't let go.

00:04:04.944 --> 00:04:10.083
If they don't understand, they will not
stop pestering you with annoying questions.

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:12.418
But sometimes those colleagues
actually help you

00:04:12.418 --> 00:04:16.689
because by explaining it to them
you realize, Oh yeah.

00:04:16.756 --> 00:04:18.558
Oh, it's simpler than I thought.

00:04:18.558 --> 00:04:21.828
This is particularly famous example
by Peter Scholze.

00:04:21.928 --> 00:04:24.931
He had a very, very difficult proof
that he wasn't 100% sure of.

00:04:25.031 --> 00:04:26.966
He would try and say, well, this is true

00:04:26.966 --> 00:04:29.288
and you should know this Lean and Lean would say,

00:04:29.288 --> 00:04:31.368
I didn't know this at all.

00:04:31.368 --> 00:04:33.007
You're going to have to explain this better.

00:04:33.007 --> 00:04:35.241
The bits where he had been uncomfortable
with what he'd written,

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:36.976
it was where it asked the most questions.

00:04:36.976 --> 00:04:40.913
So he felt okay, that was verification
and it was sensibly

00:04:41.014 --> 00:04:43.016
asking the right questions.

00:04:43.116 --> 00:04:45.285
So it's an exciting time.

00:04:45.285 --> 00:04:49.389
The question is, will machines
change mathematics?

00:04:49.489 --> 00:04:53.826
How do you get computers
to lead you in a proof,

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:58.364
not just follow you or make suggestions,
but prove things itself?

00:04:58.464 --> 00:04:59.899
This is something in its infancy,

00:04:59.899 --> 00:05:03.069
a lot of the work on computer
generated proofs up to now

00:05:03.069 --> 00:05:06.339
has not done much of consequence.

00:05:06.406 --> 00:05:09.409
Very interesting within itself,
but not in the bigger picture.

00:05:09.609 --> 00:05:12.045
But there are some very interesting,
exciting new ideas,

00:05:12.045 --> 00:05:16.382
which one has some optimism
that we may see

00:05:16.382 --> 00:05:20.753
the start of generating
interesting new proofs?

00:05:20.853 --> 00:05:22.789
And so this starts
to become a little frightening,

00:05:22.789 --> 00:05:24.357
I mean, we value our profession.

00:05:24.357 --> 00:05:27.438
We value our work by the profundity of proofs.

00:05:27.438 --> 00:05:28.328
But if we can

00:05:28.461 --> 00:05:32.131
rely on a machine to get all the details
right, well, most of the details right,

00:05:32.198 --> 00:05:35.068
then who are we? What is our training?

00:05:35.068 --> 00:05:37.403
What is it that we value?

00:05:37.503 --> 00:05:40.673
So when we don't need to think about proof,

00:05:40.773 --> 00:05:43.476
then we're not going to be trained
in thinking about proof.

00:05:43.476 --> 00:05:44.644
So who are we going to become?

00:05:44.644 --> 00:05:46.245
We're going to become more like physicists,
probably

00:05:46.245 --> 00:05:50.350
and say any old nonsense, and just hope the computer verifies it.

00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:52.985
I think it's very hard to predict
what even will be the point

00:05:52.985 --> 00:05:57.423
of doing mathematics in 30 or 40 years time.

00:05:57.490 --> 00:06:00.360
The idea of computer generated proofs

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:03.229
these present new possibilities.

00:06:03.229 --> 00:06:08.234
Over time, we see, of course, more and
more things that computers are able to do,

00:06:08.234 --> 00:06:10.234
and its very unclear what limits there are.

